WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.536
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.836 --> 00:00:09.420
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:17.225
Unit four: Economic Theory. Today you will condense economic processes into compact abstract noun phrases.

4
00:00:17.525 --> 00:00:20.247
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.259
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:25.117 --> 00:00:26.292
Supply-side.

7
00:00:27.283 --> 00:00:32.348
Supply-side means focused on boosting production through lower taxes and regulation.

8
00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:36.679
Supply-side policies aim to encourage investment.

9
00:00:37.283 --> 00:00:40.226
Your turn. Say: Supply-side.

10
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:44.393
Supply-side.

11
00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:46.325
Demand-side.

12
00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:51.510
Demand-side means focused on boosting spending by consumers and government.

13
00:00:52.117 --> 00:00:55.312
Demand-side stimulus was used during the recession.

14
00:00:55.917 --> 00:00:58.325
Your turn. Say: Demand-side.

15
00:01:01.167 --> 00:01:02.830
Demand-side.

16
00:01:03.583 --> 00:01:04.875
Fiscal policy.

17
00:01:05.883 --> 00:01:10.484
Fiscal policy means government decisions on taxes and public spending.

18
00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:13.849
Fiscal policy was tightened to cut the deficit.

19
00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:17.230
Your turn. Say: Fiscal policy.

20
00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:21.532
Fiscal policy.

21
00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:23.911
Monetary policy.

22
00:01:24.917 --> 00:01:29.703
Monetary policy means central-bank control of interest rates and money supply.

23
00:01:30.317 --> 00:01:33.500
Monetary policy reacts to rising inflation.

24
00:01:34.117 --> 00:01:37.129
Your turn. Say: Monetary policy.

25
00:01:39.967 --> 00:01:41.467
Monetary policy.

26
00:01:42.217 --> 00:01:43.821
Market equilibrium.

27
00:01:44.817 --> 00:01:48.465
Market equilibrium means the point where supply equals demand.

28
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.164
Prices adjust until the market reaches equilibrium.

29
00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.867
Your turn. Say: Market equilibrium.

30
00:01:58.733 --> 00:02:00.524
Market equilibrium.

31
00:02:01.283 --> 00:02:02.679
Externality.

32
00:02:03.683 --> 00:02:08.609
Externality means a cost or benefit that affects people outside a transaction.

33
00:02:09.217 --> 00:02:11.936
Pollution is a classic negative externality.

34
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:15.109
Your turn. Say: Externality.

35
00:02:17.967 --> 00:02:19.328
Externality.

36
00:02:20.083 --> 00:02:21.781
Opportunity cost.

37
00:02:22.783 --> 00:02:27.116
Opportunity cost means the value of the best alternative you give up.

38
00:02:27.717 --> 00:02:30.691
The opportunity cost of university is lost income.

39
00:02:31.283 --> 00:02:34.864
Your turn. Say: Opportunity cost.

40
00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.328
Opportunity cost.

41
00:02:40.083 --> 00:02:41.491
Economic model.

42
00:02:42.483 --> 00:02:46.898
Economic model means a simplified representation of how an economy works.

43
00:02:47.483 --> 00:02:50.145
Every economic model rests on assumptions.

44
00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:53.762
Your turn. Say: Economic model.

45
00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.077
Economic model.

46
00:02:58.817 --> 00:03:00.096
Inflation rate.

47
00:03:01.083 --> 00:03:04.894
Inflation rate means the percentage increase in prices over a year.

48
00:03:05.483 --> 00:03:07.947
The inflation rate rose to six per cent.

49
00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:11.295
Your turn. Say: Inflation rate.

50
00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:15.552
Inflation rate.

51
00:03:16.317 --> 00:03:17.306
Recession.

52
00:03:18.317 --> 00:03:22.127
Recession means a significant decline in economic activity.

53
00:03:22.717 --> 00:03:25.378
The recession led to widespread job losses.

54
00:03:25.983 --> 00:03:29.007
Your turn. Say: Recession.

55
00:03:31.867 --> 00:03:32.995
Recession.

56
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:37.137
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:37.983 --> 00:03:44.688
Visal, your essay says 'when supply and demand balance, the price is set'. Could you make that more academic?

58
00:03:45.783 --> 00:03:55.425
Yes: the equilibrium of supply and demand determines price. The process becomes a noun phrase, so the sentence is shorter and more formal.

59
00:03:56.517 --> 00:04:00.342
Good. Now explain why the central bank raised interest rates.

60
00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:10.137
The persistent rise in the inflation rate prompted a tightening of monetary policy, in order to cool consumer demand.

61
00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:12.425
And the critics?

62
00:04:13.517 --> 00:04:22.064
They argue that the reduction in borrowing could trigger a recession, so the opportunity cost of fighting inflation might be very high.

63
00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:24.549
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:25.383 --> 00:04:28.219
Grammar focus: Abstract nominalization.

65
00:04:28.519 --> 00:04:29.717
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:30.417 --> 00:04:33.717
The equilibrium of supply and demand determines price.

67
00:04:34.617 --> 00:04:38.555
The persistent rise in the inflation rate prompted a change in policy.

68
00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:42.344
A reduction in borrowing could trigger a recession.

69
00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:54.230
Turn clauses into noun phrases: 'prices rose steadily' → 'the steady rise in prices'. Note the prepositions: a rise / fall / reduction IN something; the effect OF something.

70
00:04:54.983 --> 00:04:59.168
Be careful! Don't say: The rise of inflation rate worried investors.

71
00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:05.002
Say: The rise in the inflation rate worried investors.

72
00:05:05.302 --> 00:05:07.046
Use 'a rise in' something.

73
00:05:07.833 --> 00:05:10.323
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:12.738
Models and Reality.

75
00:05:13.038 --> 00:05:18.230
Every economic model simplifies reality in order to explain one relationship clearly.

76
00:05:19.233 --> 00:05:25.053
The assumption of rational consumers, for example, allows economists to predict the effect of a price change.

77
00:05:26.067 --> 00:05:31.967
Yet the persistence of financial crises suggests that such assumptions are, at best, approximations.

78
00:05:32.967 --> 00:05:39.506
Externalities further complicate the picture, since the true cost of production is often borne by third parties.

79
00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:46.935
Consequently, the value of a model lies less in its accuracy than in the questions it allows us to ask.

80
00:05:48.033 --> 00:05:51.989
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:56.127
Choose the nominalized version of 'prices rose sharply'.

82
00:05:56.427 --> 00:06:02.437
A, prices' sharp rising. B, the sharply rise of prices. C, the sharp rise in prices.

83
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.894
The answer is C: the sharp rise in prices.

84
00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:13.002
A blank in interest rates usually reduces borrowing.

85
00:06:13.302 --> 00:06:16.769
A, rise. B, rising up. C, raise up.

86
00:06:20.333 --> 00:06:22.356
The answer is A: rise.

87
00:06:23.517 --> 00:06:26.445
The value of the best alternative you give up is the blank.

88
00:06:26.745 --> 00:06:31.734
A, externality. B, opportunity cost. C, equilibrium.

89
00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:38.019
The answer is B: opportunity cost.

90
00:06:39.183 --> 00:06:42.924
Pollution that harms nearby residents is an example of a negative blank.

91
00:06:43.224 --> 00:06:47.679
A, externality. B, recession. C, mandate.

92
00:06:51.233 --> 00:06:53.744
The answer is A: externality.

93
00:06:54.883 --> 00:06:58.764
According to the reading, a model's value lies mainly in…

94
00:06:59.064 --> 00:07:05.097
A, its perfect accuracy. B, the questions it lets us ask. C, its rational consumers.

95
00:07:08.633 --> 00:07:11.446
The answer is B: the questions it lets us ask.

96
00:07:12.633 --> 00:07:15.669
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:16.517 --> 00:07:19.654
Which economic theory do you find most convincing?

98
00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:37.869
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:38.169 --> 00:08:12.397
Personally, I find a balanced Keynesian approach the most convincing. During a recession, the collapse in private spending can be offset by government investment, which protects jobs. The response to the 2008 crisis, for instance, showed how fiscal stimulus can shorten a downturn. Supply-side economists argue, however, that high public spending leads to debt and weak growth later. I accept that risk, but I believe stability should come first in a crisis.

100
00:08:12.697 --> 00:08:14.534
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:20.192
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and abstract nominalization.

102
00:08:20.492 --> 00:08:26.500
Today's value is economic awareness. Understand how money and work affect your life choices.

103
00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:29.231
See you in Unit five. Goodbye!

104
00:08:29.531 --> 00:08:32.358
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

